pra: CMD18 multi-block reads by default - the printed sector ladder

The old 0142 wedge (a multi-block transfer aborted with a soft reset but
no CMD12 left the card mid-stream, DAT asserted, every later command
answered "card busy" until the next power cycle) was fixed by 0148 and
never re-tested: every boot since ran with emmcmulti=0, paying ~137 us
of command setup plus ~42 us of port drain per 512-byte sector (ring19:
"wait 140 us buf 42 us" at 2.7 MB/s), capping Setup's page-ins at
per-sector rates while the system burned 80% CPU in PIO copy loops.

- Reads issue CMD18 chunks that climb a printed ladder (2 -> 8 -> 16 ->
  32 -> 64 -> 128 sectors; one rung per success, a failure resets it,
  three consecutive NON-BUSY failures latch the path off for the boot -
  a busy card is transient and must never count).
- PRA_MULTI_MAX corrected 64 -> 128 sectors (the old comment's 64 KiB
  arithmetic was wrong: 64 sectors is 32 KiB) with the per-chunk
  wait/buf stats normalized per sector so they stay comparable with the
  CMD17 baseline.
- emmcmulti=2 disables auto CMD12 and stops chunks explicitly - the
  diagnostic that separates "the wedge" from "multi-block itself" if
  the ladder ever wedges again.
- The canonical cmdline drops emmcmulti=0 and both gates drop it from
  the critical-arg list (12 -> 11); it remains a one-off escape hatch.

From 0001-pra-emmc-mbfix Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
Subject: [PRA eMMC 0001] reliable CMD18 multi-block reads with a printed
 chunk ladder; make multi the default and drop emmcmulti=0 from the gates

DRAFT - not applied, not committed. Stacks on the existing 0139..0161
series in xnu/patches/. Verify on hardware per
research/driver-specs/emmc-work/WORKLOG.md before it becomes canonical.

WHY (measured, ring19/ring18, all boots with emmcmulti=0):

Per-command overhead, not the bus, is the read bottleneck. Healthy
intervals (ring19:112-204) show 8 MiB in 16,464 commands at 2,753 KiB/s
with "wait 137-144 us buf 42-43 us": every 512-byte CMD17 pays ~137 us of
command setup + card access latency and then the CPU drains 128 32-bit
words from the buffer port at ~328 ns/word (~42 us). That is ~190 us per
sector = the 2.7 MB/s ceiling, and it burns CPU in poll loops the whole
time (~1.4 ms CPU per 4 KiB page-in; the 80% CPU / 2.8%-Setup share seen
by the sampler is mostly this plus page faults). The card itself is
innocent: EXT_CSD reports hs_timing=3 / bus_width=6 / device_type=0x57
(ring19:6) - an HS200/HS400-class handoff - and the caps register
(0x44ecc881) says base clock 200 MHz with div=0.

The contradiction recorded in PRA-LX1-noted.md:1233 is resolved here: the
PRA_MULTI_MAX comment ("per-command overhead dominant, CMD18 the lever")
is right; the 0146-era note ("multi-block buys ~15%") was an inference
from the 0142 wedge, never a measurement. Ring evidence gives no support
for "at the card's internal limit": the fixed 137 us is command latency,
which CMD18 amortises; after the first block the card streams at the port
floor (~12 MB/s with 128-word drains, ~42 us/block).

WHY CMD18 WEDGED (0142) AND WHY IT IS SAFE NOW:

0142 introduced CMD18 with two defects, both fixed by 0148 but never
re-enabled in the canonical cmdline (no ring after 0148 shows the multi
path running - every ring prints "CMD18 disabled by boot arg"):
  1. NORMAL_BUFFER_READ_READY was never cleared between blocks, so blocks
     1..N drained the port before the controller had the next block - a
     desynchronised PIO stream and bad data (pra_sdhci.c:484-489 today).
  2. The error path called pra_emmc_abort_data() (soft reset CMD|DAT)
     with no CMD12, so the card kept driving DAT mid-stream; PRESENT_STATE
     DAT-inhibit stayed set and every later command returned
     "card busy, skip read" EBUSY until power cycle. That is the
     0146 suspicion verbatim (PRA-LX1-noted.md:988-995) and the mount
     failures that forced emmcmulti=0 into tools/pra_package.sh (its
     cmdline gate, pra_flash_skip.sh's gate too).

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES:

- pra_sdhci.c: the chunk size grows on a printed ladder
  2 -> 8 -> 16 -> 32 -> 64 -> 128 sectors (PRA_MULTI_MAX 64 -> 128, so
  the comment's "64 KiB" arithmetic finally matches: 128 sectors =
  64 KiB). A failed chunk resets the ladder and finishes the request on
  the per-sector path; three consecutive non-EBUSY failures latch CMD18
  off for the boot with one print (EBUSY never counts - transient). The
  first wrong guess costs 1 KiB, not a boot.
- read_multi gains per-chunk stats wired into the existing wait/buf
  counters, divided by sectors, so the printed per-sector averages stay
  comparable with the CMD17 baseline (wait per sector should drop from
  ~140 us to a few us once chunks reach the top rungs; buf stays ~42 us
  until 0002's SDMA).
- emmcmulti=2: explicit CMD12 stop mode (auto CMD12 bit cleared,
  pra_emmc_stop_transfer() before waiting transfer complete) - the
  diagnostic that separates "auto CMD12 is broken" from "CMD18 is broken"
  if the wedge reappears.
- DKIOCGETMAXBLOCKCOUNTREAD now reports the chunk size (was hard 1), so
  HFS stops splitting its cluster reads.
- tools/pra_package.sh + tools/pra_flash_skip.sh: emmcmulti=0 removed
  from the canonical CMDLINE and from the critical-args hard gates in the
  SAME change (12 -> 11 args); emmcmulti=0/2 stay documented escape
  hatches for one-off CMDLINE experiments.

RISK: the 0148-fixed CMD18 path has never run on this hardware (no ring
shows it); the ladder bounds whatever is still wrong to one small chunk
per failure and three printed failures before fallback. UNKNOWN: whether
the Arasan's auto CMD12 behaves at handoff state (emmcmulti=2 answers
that), and whether multi-block reads behave under the write-storm load
that collapsed ring17 (ring17:15882: 15,328 CMD24 writes per interval).
